{"name":"Arrow Shaft","hex":"#5c503a","fetched":"2026-10-11","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"arrow","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A projectile consisting of a shaft, a point and a tail with stabilizing fins that is shot from a bow.","A sign or symbol used to indicate a direction (e.g.→)."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To move swiftly and directly (like an arrow).","To let fly swiftly and directly."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/arrow"},{"term":"shaft","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The entire body of a long weapon, such as an arrow.","The long, narrow, central body of a spear, arrow, or javelin."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To fuck over; to cause harm to, especially through deceit or treachery.","To equip with a shaft."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/shaft"}],"entries":[{"title":"Arrow","blocks":[{"text":"An arrow is a fin-stabilized projectile launched by a bow. A typical arrow usually consists of a long, stiff, straight shaft with a weighty arrowhead attached to the front end, multiple fin-like stabilizers called fletchings mounted near the rear, and a slot at the rear end called a nock for engaging the bowstring. A container or bag carrying additional arrows for convenient reloading is called a quiver."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/86/DFRArrow.jpg/330px-DFRArrow.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Arrow"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Two_arrows.jpg/500px-Two_arrows.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Two_arrows.jpg/1280px-Two_arrows.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Two_arrows.jpg/500px-Two_arrows.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia